## What "Interactive Play" Means in Practice

The term gets used loosely, so let's be specific. At Infusion Play, Interactive Play is a set of physical challenge rooms where the environment itself is the obstacle. No screens, no headsets — just your body, your coordination, and sometimes your memory or pattern recognition to get through each challenge.

The rooms are built to push different skills. Some are pure reflex and movement. Others slow you down and make you think. Most groups cycle through both types in a single session, which is part of why the format holds up across different ages and fitness levels.

Points are tracked throughout and redeemable for prizes at the end, so there's a competitive thread running through the whole experience. That detail matters more than it sounds — especially for groups where half the people are genuinely competitive and the other half just want to have a good time.

