## The Lit Floor Grid: What It Feels Like

Picture a floor that lights up in patterns and sequences. Your job is to respond fast enough — step on the right tiles, avoid the wrong ones, match a sequence before it resets.

It sounds simple. It is not simple.

The first few rounds feel manageable. The grid moves at a pace you can track, and your feet find the right tiles without much stress. Then the speed picks up, the pattern shifts, or two players are competing on the same grid and suddenly you're both scrambling for the same lit section.

What your body actually does: quick lateral steps, weight shifts, a lot of stopping short and redirecting. It's more athletic than most people expect from something that looks like a floor game. Your calves and hips do most of the work, and your eyes are reading the grid constantly — a low-grade mental load that compounds with the physical effort.

For groups, the lit floor grid becomes its own social event. People shout instructions at each other, celebrate hits, groan at misses. The points system gives every round a stakes level that keeps everyone engaged even when they're watching from the side.

### Who Handles It Best

The age range is wide. Teenagers tend to dominate on raw speed. Adults with good spatial awareness often do better on pattern-recognition rounds. Younger kids who can follow the rules generally love it because the feedback is instant and obvious. If you're bringing children under about 8, the sister location — Infusion XR Arcade — is the better fit for age-appropriate room-scale experiences.

